<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claudius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10 BCE–54 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Tiberius Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claudius was Roman emperor from 41 to 54 CE, proclaimed by the Praetorian Guard after the murder of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>. His reign saw the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 43 and the organisation of Mauretania, Lycia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Judea as provinces, and he admitted Gallic nobles to the Senate. He was probably poisoned by his wife Agrippina, who secured the throne for her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudius.jpg" alt="Claudius" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with Rome, and according to Tacitus, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, Antiochus III, Aristonicus, the pirates and Mithridates VI of Pontus; <b>Claudius</b> responded by remitting their tribute for five years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Romans installed Mithridates of Armenia, whom Caligula arrested and <b>Claudius</b> restored; the Parthians forced submission in 37 CE, Rome retook the kingdom in 47 CE, Rhadamistus' Iberians seized it in 51 CE and Tigranes VI ruled from 58 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Rhoemetalces III was murdered, the emperor <b>Claudius</b> annexed the kingdom in 46 CE as the province of Thracia, ending native kingship in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 44 CE <b>Claudius</b> withdrew Rhodian freedom after Roman citizens were executed there; it was restored in 53 CE but lost for good under Vespasian, when Rhodes was absorbed into the Roman provincial system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 43 CE the emperor <b>Claudius</b>, seeking military prestige, sent Aulus Plautius with four legions, the II Augusta, IX Hispana, XIV Gemina and XX, and auxiliaries, perhaps 40,000 men in all.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The consul Publius <b>Claudius</b> Pulcher, in 249 BCE, threw the sacred chickens into the sea when they would not eat before a naval battle, “saying that they might drink, since they would not eat”; he was defeated, and his impiety ruined his career.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
